[38;2;124;124;124mthere are infinitely many prime ideals p in C. This is a classic result in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124malgebraic number theory - the generalization of Dirichlet's theorem on primes in[0m[38;2;124;124;124marithmetic progressions to prime ideals in ideal classes.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think through the answer and proof.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe answer is yes. This is the generalized Dirichlet theorem / Chebotarev-style [0m[38;2;124;124;124mresult. The key ingredients:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m1. The narrow class group Cl^+(K) is finite.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. The generalized Dirichlet theorem (or a consequence of Chebotarev density [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtheorem, or the classical analytic proof generalizing Dirichlet) states that [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mprime ideals are equidistributed among narrow ideal classes.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me be careful. The classical result is about ordinary ideal [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mclasses. The question is about narrow ideal classes.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe narrow class group Cl^+(K) is the group of fractional ideals modulo [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mprincipal ideals generated by totally positive elements (elements positive at [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mevery real embedding).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe claim: every narrow ideal class contains infinitely many prime ideals.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is true. The proof uses Hecke L-functions associated to characters of the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnarrow class group.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me sketch the proof properly.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mProof sketch:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet h^+ = |Cl^+(K)| be the narrow class number. Let χ be a character of Cl^+(K).[0m[38;2;124;124;124mWe can define a Hecke L-function:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mL(s, χ) = ∏_{p} (1 - χ(p) N(p)^{-s})^{-1}[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mwhere the product is over prime ideals p, and χ(p) is the value of χ on the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnarrow class of p (for unramified primes; ramified primes handled separately).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor the trivial character, L(s, χ_0) is essentially the Dedekind zeta function [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mζ_K(s) (up to finitely many Euler factors), which has a pole at s = 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor non-trivial characters χ, L(s, χ) is holomorphic and non-vanishing at s = 1.[0m[38;2;124;124;124mThis is the key non-vanishing result, proved analogously to Dirichlet's original[0m[38;2;124;124;124margument (or via more modern techniques).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, using orthogonality of characters: the number of primes p with N(p) ≤ x in [0m[38;2;124;124;124ma fixed narrow class C is:[0m

